You can use any object directly in an if statement without explicitly comparing it to True or False. This is a common Python idiom. For example, checking whether a list has items can be done by testing the list itself rather than its length. Similarly, checking whether a string contains text is done by testing the string directly. Here is how you can use truthiness in practice:

```python
def process_items(items):
    if items:
        print('Processing', len(items), 'items')
        for item in items:
            print('Item:', item)
    else:
        print('No items to process')

process_items([10, 20, 30])
process_items([])
```

In the first call, items is a non-empty list, so it is truthy and the loop executes. In the second call, items is an empty list, so it is falsy and the else branch runs.

